th1-surjection-curation-001+math1-bijection-curation-001+math1-union-curation-001+math1-proper-curation-001+math1-complement-curation-001+math1-complement-render-001+math1-division-curation-001+math1-reciprocal-curation-001+math1-inequality-curation-001+math1-inequality-feedback-001+math1-maximum-curation-001+math1-minimum-curation-001+math1-boundedset-curation-001+math1-endpoint-curation-001+audit-groups-20261027+topup-math-i-20261028+topup-math-ii-20261028+topup-pt-20261028+topup-math-iii-20261028","concept_id":"Year-1/1b-Math-II-Linear-Algebra/distinct-eigenvalues-theorem.html","concept":{"concept_id":"Year-1/1b-Math-II-Linear-Algebra/distinct-eigenvalues-theorem.html","questions":[{"id":"wiki20261002_distinct_eigenvalues_001","difficulty":"basic","question_type":"conceptual","question":"If $Av=2v$ and $Aw=3w$ with $v,w\\ne0$, what does the distinct eigenvalues theorem guarantee?","options":["The vectors are perpendicular.","The vectors are scalar multiples.","The vectors are linearly independent.","The vectors have equal length."],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"978c509c22bbfd5805536391","time_limit":25},{"id":"wiki20261002_distinct_eigenvalues_002","difficulty":"basic","question_type":"conceptual","question":"A real $3\\times3$ matrix has three distinct real eigenvalues. Which conclusion is guaranteed?","options":["It has three positive eigenvalues.","It is diagonalizable over $\\mathbb{R}$.","It is a diagonal matrix.","It is a symmetric matrix."],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"514c452d7fd6d6b8180adc70","time_limit":20},{"id":"wiki20261002_distinct_eigenvalues_004","difficulty":"basic","question_type":"application","question":"For $A=2I_2$, which statement about $v=(1,0)$ and $w=(2,0)$ is correct?","options":["They form a basis of $\\mathbb{R}^2$.","They are dependent eigenvectors.","They have distinct eigenvalues.","They are independent eigenvectors."],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"35a725eb5f7e7ce32203302a","time_limit":25},{"id":"wiki20261002_distinct_eigenvalues_005","difficulty":"basic","question_type":"application","question":"For $A=2I_2$, the vectors $(1,0)$ and $(0,1)$ are eigenvectors. What does this example show?","options":["Repeated eigenvalues can have independent eigenvectors.","Independent eigenvectors must have distinct eigenvalues.","A scalar matrix has no eigenbasis.","Repeated eigenvalues always force dependence."],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"f69634e68a8c817a84b08aa7","time_limit":25},{"id":"wiki20261002_distinct_eigenvalues_007","difficulty":"standard","question_type":"application","question":"For $A=\\begin{pmatrix}1&1\\\\0&2\\end{pmatrix}$, take $v=(1,0)$ and $w=(1,1)$. Which statement holds?","options":["They have the same eigenvalue.","They are independent but not perpendicular.","They are neither eigenvectors nor independent.","They are perpendicular and dependent."],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"7f5f713a244c3327f2dd4317","time_limit":30},{"id":"wiki20261002_distinct_eigenvalues_008","difficulty":"standard","question_type":"application","question":"A real $3\\times3$ matrix has distinct real eigenvalues $-1,2,5$ with eigenvectors $u,v,w$. If $3u+cv+dw=0$, what follows?","options":["No such scalars $c,d$ exist.","$c+d=-3$ is sufficient.","$c=d=0$ solves the equation.","$c=-3$ and $d=0$ must hold."],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"457949235a848cb373cddd18","time_limit":30},{"id":"wiki20261002_distinct_eigenvalues_010","difficulty":"standard","question_type":"conceptual","question":"A real $3\\times3$ matrix has eigenvectors for two distinct real eigenvalues. What can be concluded from those two vectors alone?","options":["The matrix has exactly two eigenvectors.","The matrix must be diagonalizable.","They form a basis of $\\mathbb{R}^3$.","They span a two-dimensional subspace."],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"28350acfb05e1e6258fd0ec2","time_limit":30},{"id":"wiki20261002_distinct_eigenvalues_012","difficulty":"standard","question_type":"application","question":"A real $2\\times2$ matrix has two distinct real eigenvalues, one of them zero. Which conclusion follows?","options":["It is singular and not diagonalizable.","It is invertible and diagonalizable.","It is invertible but not diagonalizable.","It is singular and diagonalizable."],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"495b16e794267ffa0b9452e1","time_limit":30},{"id":"wiki20261002_distinct_eigenvalues_013","difficulty":"standard","question_type":"conceptual","question":"Over $\\mathbb{C}$, a $2\\times2$ matrix has eigenvalues $\\mathrm{i}$ and $-\\mathrm{i}$. What follows for corresponding eigenvectors?","options":["They must both belong to $\\mathbb{R}^2$.","They must have eigenvalue zero as well.","They are linearly independent over $\\mathbb{C}$.","They are scalar multiples over $\\mathbb{C}$."],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"dafb0bb5cf7d694e7ccc77f7","time_limit":30},{"id":"wiki20261002_distinct_eigenvalues_014","difficulty":"standard","question_type":"application","question":"Over $\\mathbb{R}$, $A(t)=\\operatorname{diag}(1,t,3)$. For which $t$ does the distinct eigenvalues theorem directly guarantee an eigenbasis?","options":["Only $t>3$.","$t\\notin\\{1,3\\}$.","Every real $t$.","Only $t=1$ or $t=3$."],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"4e5cd4ca8fc15fd15f0ad7a5","time_limit":30},{"id":"wiki20261002_distinct_eigenvalues_015","difficulty":"standard","question_type":"conceptual","question":"Over $\\mathbb{R}$, eigenvectors $v,w$ have distinct eigenvalues $\\lambda,\\mu$. Which conclusion about $u=v+w$ is correct?","options":["It is not an eigenvector.","It has eigenvalue $(\\lambda+\\mu)/2$.","It has eigenvalue $\\lambda+\\mu$.","It is necessarily the zero vector."],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"2ffe0eb9c1f278d4fb4a7928","time_limit":30},{"id":"wiki20261002_distinct_eigenvalues_016","difficulty":"standard","question_type":"application","question":"Over $\\mathbb{R}$, $Av=\\lambda v$ and $Aw=\\mu w$ for distinct nonzero eigenvalues and nonzero eigenvectors. What is $(A-\\mu I)v$?","options":["$(\\lambda-\\mu)v$","$0$","$(\\lambda+\\mu)v$","$(\\mu-\\lambda)v$"],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"bdf84f842c0c5b43c0bcc371","time_limit":30},{"id":"wiki20261002_distinct_eigenvalues_017","difficulty":"challenge","question_type":"application","question":"A real $3\\times3$ matrix has eigenvalues $1,2,3$ and eigenvectors $v_1,v_2,v_3$. Let $P=[v_1\\ v_2\\ v_3]$. Which identity holds?","options":["$P^{-1}AP=I_3$","$P^{-1}AP=\\operatorname{diag}(1,2,3)$","$P^{-1}AP=\\operatorname{diag}(3,2,1)$","$P^{-1}AP=0$"],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"6dfa14d85edd0a39647d84d1","time_limit":40},{"id":"wiki20261002_distinct_eigenvalues_019","difficulty":"challenge","question_type":"conceptual","question":"A linear operator on a real space of dimension four has five distinct real eigenvalues. Which assessment is correct?","options":["The fifth eigenvalue must be ignored.","It must have five independent columns.","It has an eigenbasis of five vectors.","Such an operator cannot exist."],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"2b231f51097511b67a30d91e","time_limit":40}]}}